WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.460
Protective bonding and the direct-current return are different functions.

2
00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:08.960
Protective earth and chassis bonding establish protective connections among accessible conductive parts.

3
00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:12.560
The D C return carries operating current in the power circuit.

4
00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:16.000
A common word such as ground can hide that distinction.

5
00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:19.880
The high-power shelf implementation includes a specific grounding scheme.

6
00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:24.160
Read that scheme and the applicable rack requirements together; don't invent a

7
00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:27.700
connection between the return and chassis simply because the frame is bonded.

8
00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:31.380
A simplified diagram that merges the two paths would teach the wrong

9
00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:32.000
mental model.

10
00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:37.060
In an integration review, identify the conductors, their intended functions, the connection

11
00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:39.220
points and the responsible design authority.

12
00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:44.000
Verify continuity and fault behavior through the approved engineering and acceptance process.

13
00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:47.720
This course doesn't provide installation or live-work instructions.

14
00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:51.580
The key decision is to stop an ambiguous grounding claim before it

15
00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:53.520
becomes an undocumented wiring choice.

16
00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:58.400
If the proposed assembly differs from the cited implementation, obtain a design-specific

17
00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:01.700
review rather than assuming the original diagram still applies.

